WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:03.340
I think that, and I talk about this in
some depth in the book,

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:06.000
there are two misperceptions people,

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.100
even very brilliant people, labor under

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.920
that I think are really going to keep us from

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.400
imagining memory the way we knew we need to.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.360
The first is this period of information inflation

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:16.760
that we're living through.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:21.400
People think that it's created by computers and that

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.080
it's the first time in history that this has ever happened.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.660
This is a moment that's never happened before

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:30.000
Wrong on two counts. Every information technology

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.540
when it's new creates a kind of vertigo.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:35.220
In this case it's a digital vertigo but, as I'll show you,

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.940
there's always a vertigo. And the other

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:40.900
misconception we have is about memory itself.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.540
Because memory is not data storage.

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:47.720
Memory in fact is a biological feature of all creatures.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:51.980
And the first step in making robust memories over time

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:55.520
is to forget. And the reason that we have memory

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.220
to begin with, that nature has endowed all creatures

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:00.420
with it, is because memory is not about the past.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.480
It is about the future. If we were born only with DNA

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.160
we'd all perish second or third day in life.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:11.800
And that's true even of amoebas. So with that,

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.980
I want to start with talking a little bit about technology.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.642
Many of you will recognize this is one of the first

00:01:17.642 --> 00:01:22.380
and certainly the most durable information technology
that humans have created.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.778
It's a clay tablet also known as a cuneiform.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:30.480
Technology is a tool that's created by human beings.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.287
Technologies have no powers of their own.

00:01:33.287 --> 00:01:35.140
They only have the powers that we

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.220
endow those technologies with.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.811
And the technologies especially around memory

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:42.380
are usually invented to solve a problem

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:44.500
or to meet some kind of a market demand.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.420
Now about 5000 years ago when the scribes

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:50.400
in Mesopotamia invented these clay tablets

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.840
what they were responding to was the need

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:57.500
to keep track of goods and services
in exchange or in storage.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:01.020
So they were basically accountants that invented writing

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.500
but within a relatively short period of time

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.840
a couple thousand years
--in Sumerian time that's relatively quick--

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.180
the technology began to acquire

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.849
lots of different capacities and develop

00:02:11.849 --> 00:02:13.770
certain potentials in human beings that were

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.043
totally unanticipated by the accountants.

00:02:16.043 --> 00:02:18.150
Another lesson for technologists today,

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:20.680
if they think they understand anything about the uses

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:22.680
the future uses of what they create.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:27.140
In fact what they came up with fairly quickly,

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:30.900
and this tablet is about 2700 years old,

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.590
is uses including prayers and omens.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:36.660
If you could actually read this...

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:38.540
I just want to point out incidentally that

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:42.720
like the Rosetta Stone this is actually eye legible

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.780
it's a wonderful technology we can still read it today.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:50.520
And if you did read Akkadian...
Does anybody in the audience read Akkadian?

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.420
Good. I'll get no quizzes on what it says.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:58.800
But I do know that it's filled with divinations
about omens from animals

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.760
and human birth defects telling us about
what the future is going to be.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:09.380
So if you were able to read this you would know

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:12.780
that in fact cuneiforms were very quickly recruited to

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:14.940
do something that accountants never dreamt about.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.524
The first recorded poem that we have,

00:03:17.524 --> 00:03:20.610
the epic Gilgamesh, uses this technology.

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:23.441
And as you may know Gilgamesh is actually an epic

00:03:23.441 --> 00:03:26.340
about a man who wanted to become immortal.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.140
So, very appropriate.

